WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.478
A cognitive radio must find free channels fast, and more and more often it runs on a small, low-power device.

2
00:00:07.175 --> 00:00:14.118
This video shows how quantization-aware training lets deep-learning spectrum sensing run on such a device.

3
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:23.538
Mobile and Internet-of-Things devices keep multiplying, and a static allocation of the radio spectrum no longer keeps up.

4
00:00:23.117 --> 00:00:30.420
Cognitive radio lets secondary users borrow channels that the licensed primary users leave empty, without interfering with them.

5
00:00:31.372 --> 00:00:34.170
These empty channels are called spectrum holes.

6
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:39.099
To catch as many holes as possible, a secondary user monitors a wide band at once.

7
00:00:39.796 --> 00:00:46.715
This is wideband spectrum sensing, and because it must keep up with a high bandwidth, latency matters most.

8
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.448
The band from zero to B is divided into M sub-bands.

9
00:00:51.145 --> 00:00:57.867
Each primary user occupies one of them, and the occupancy is a vector y of M zeros and ones.

10
00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:07.170
The secondary user receives the sum of the primary users' signals plus Gaussian noise, and samples it into in-phase and quadrature components, I and Q.

11
00:01:06.957 --> 00:01:12.147
A window of N samples becomes a matrix X with N rows and two columns.

12
00:01:12.843 --> 00:01:16.988
A network f maps each window to one score per sub-band.

13
00:01:16.637 --> 00:01:19.737
A score of at least one half marks a sub-band as occupied.

14
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:26.714
Sensing thus becomes an M-label classification, with up to two to the M occupancy patterns.

15
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:31.302
Convolutional networks learn this mapping directly from raw I/Q samples.

16
00:01:31.906 --> 00:01:41.194
DeepSense detects several spectrum holes at once, and ParallelCNN lowers latency further by processing two halves of the window in parallel.

17
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:42.799
Edge devices, however, are tight.

18
00:01:43.495 --> 00:01:52.725
Their processors run at sixty to four hundred megahertz, they have two hundred fifty-six kilobytes to four megabytes of memory, and they run on milliwatts.

19
00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:59.285
Networks stored as thirty-two-bit floats cost memory, computation and time that such devices lack.

20
00:02:00.237 --> 00:02:04.173
The paper closes this gap with quantization-aware training.

21
00:02:04.397 --> 00:02:08.658
Quantization maps floating-point values onto a small set of fixed-point levels.

22
00:02:09.354 --> 00:02:14.939
Here, weights and activations go from thirty-two-bit floats to eight-bit integers.

23
00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:23.778
Each tensor has a scale s and a zero-point z, and a real value x is approximately s times the integer x-int minus z.

24
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:31.281
To quantize, x is divided by s, rounded, shifted by z, and clamped between q-min and q-max.

25
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:34.470
The scale spreads that range over two to the eight minus one steps.

26
00:02:35.166 --> 00:02:38.022
A wider range means coarser steps.

27
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:41.544
Quantizing a model after training often costs accuracy.

28
00:02:42.241 --> 00:02:48.301
Quantization-aware training instead simulates integer arithmetic while the model trains.

29
00:02:47.783 --> 00:02:52.810
In the forward pass, inputs, weights and activations are fake-quantized:

30
00:02:53.193 --> 00:02:56.235
rounded to eight bits, then turned back into floats.

31
00:02:56.932 --> 00:03:01.622
The rounding error acts as noise, and the network learns to tolerate it.

32
00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:06.966
Rounding has no useful gradient, so the backward pass uses the straight-through estimator.

33
00:03:07.663 --> 00:03:13.073
The gradient passes unchanged inside the range, and is zero outside it.

34
00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.507
Both networks are adapted for integer hardware.

35
00:03:17.203 --> 00:03:23.299
LeakyReLU becomes ReLU6, which confines activations to between zero and six.

36
00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:31.814
One-dimensional convolution and pooling layers become two-dimensional ones, so the input window is reshaped to one by N by two.

37
00:03:31.403 --> 00:03:38.346
ParallelCNN splits the window in time into two halves, X one and X two, one per branch.

38
00:03:39.158 --> 00:03:45.242
Both branches start from identical weights, to limit how quantization noise propagates.

39
00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:47.684
The models are evaluated on two public datasets.

40
00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:56.693
The SDR dataset holds about five hundred thousand recorded WiFi transmissions over four channels, so M is four.

41
00:03:56.323 --> 00:04:10.348
The LTE dataset simulates about five hundred twenty thousand LTE-M uplink transmissions over sixteen sub-bands, with fading and noise, at signal-to-noise ratios from minus twenty to twenty decibels.

42
00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.536
Both are z-score normalized.

43
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:22.114
This steadies training under quantization noise, and it aligns the I and Q distributions, so that one static quantizer can serve the input.

44
00:04:23.183 --> 00:04:25.795
Next, the input needs a quantization range.

45
00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:33.075
It can span the full range of the normalized data, or be clipped to the central ninety-nine point nine percent.

46
00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:34.405
Clipping gives finer steps.

47
00:04:35.218 --> 00:04:43.786
For SDR, it narrows the range from about minus ten point four to nine point two, down to about plus or minus four point five.

48
00:04:44.233 --> 00:04:53.149
SDR models work best with the clipped range, but LTE models work best with the full range, across window sizes and noise levels.

49
00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.442
The reason lies in the tails.

50
00:04:55.139 --> 00:05:03.382
LTE's kurtosis is nine point nine eight, against four point one six for SDR, which points to heavier tails.

51
00:05:03.007 --> 00:05:05.526
Clipping therefore costs LTE more.

52
00:05:06.223 --> 00:05:16.614
Its information retention ratio, the variance before clipping over the variance after, is one point one six, against one point zero five for SDR.

53
00:05:16.897 --> 00:05:25.999
Table one gives the F1-scores, averaged over five folds, for both models and both datasets, at ten decibels for LTE.

54
00:05:25.493 --> 00:05:35.153
With windows of one hundred twenty-eight samples, every quantized model keeps an F1-score above zero point nine eight, close to its full-precision version.

55
00:05:34.797 --> 00:05:37.688
With thirty-two samples, quantization costs more.

56
00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:46.082
The largest drop is ParallelCNN on LTE, from zero point eight six to zero point seven one.

57
00:05:45.697 --> 00:05:54.613
A short window carries less information about the signal, and ParallelCNN halves it again, so each branch sees only sixteen samples.

58
00:05:54.897 --> 00:06:00.319
On LTE, the signal-to-noise ratio is also varied from minus twenty to twenty decibels.

59
00:06:01.015 --> 00:06:05.950
Every model improves as the signal gets cleaner, and peaks at twenty decibels.

60
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:11.649
At minus twenty decibels every model is close to random guessing, so quantization has little to lose.

61
00:06:12.345 --> 00:06:16.467
Its cost shows at moderate to high signal-to-noise ratios.

62
00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:26.957
At ten and twenty decibels, ParallelCNN with thirty-two samples falls far behind its full-precision version, while every other quantized model stays within about two points.

63
00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:35.247
ParallelCNN has few parameters and little redundancy, so the precision lost to quantization weighs more than in the larger DeepSense.

64
00:06:35.493 --> 00:06:39.231
The best model of each configuration was deployed on a Sony Spresense:

65
00:06:39.673 --> 00:06:49.843
a six-core ARM Cortex-M4F microcontroller at one hundred fifty-six megahertz, with one point five megabytes of memory and eight megabytes of flash.

66
00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:52.009
Each model ran one thousand inferences.

67
00:06:52.612 --> 00:06:59.276
In every configuration, the quantized model is smaller, faster, and draws slightly less power.

68
00:06:59.537 --> 00:07:08.918
DeepSense on LTE with one hundred twenty-eight samples shrinks by seventy-two percent, from two hundred sixty to seventy-two point six kilobytes.

69
00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:18.934
DeepSense on SDR with thirty-two samples cuts its inference time by fifty-one percent, from eleven point three to five point five milliseconds.

70
00:07:18.597 --> 00:07:27.513
Power falls by at most seven point eight percent, for ParallelCNN on SDR, and stays close to that of the standard models.

71
00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:35.898
Performance per kilobyte, the F1-score divided by the model size, rises with quantization in all eight configurations.

72
00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.107
Normalization runs on the device before every inference.

73
00:07:39.804 --> 00:07:49.289
For quantized DeepSense on SDR, it takes zero point one two milliseconds out of thirty-seven point two, with one hundred twenty-eight samples.

74
00:07:48.833 --> 00:07:53.233
With thirty-two samples, it takes zero point zero four out of five point five seven.

75
00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:57.424
Either way it stays below one percent of the latency.

76
00:07:57.713 --> 00:08:11.273
In short, quantization-aware training, hardware-friendly layers and a data-driven input range make spectrum sensing models far smaller and faster, at nearly the same F1-score when the window is long enough.

77
00:08:11.717 --> 00:08:22.305
Next steps include four-bit and mixed-precision quantization, pruning, 5G New Radio datasets, hardware-aware architecture search, and more embedded platforms.
